    "updated_at": "2019-10-28T20:04:39Z",
    "body": "Hm. So the accesskey itself might be in the element 0 or 2+ times. That makes some of the simpler solutions not workable.\r\n\r\nHere's a possible sketch:\r\n* add an `::accesskey` pseudo-element, which is text-like (not tree-abiding) and either wraps the first instance of the access key in the element's text, or the auto-generated text placed by the browser if there is none, with some default styling (underlined) that can be turned off with an `appearance: none;`. This only exists on elements with an `accesskey` attribute.\r\n* add a property that controls whether or not ::accesskey is generated at all, defaulting to \"no\" for backwards compat.\r\n* add a property that can be put on a child element to let it claim to be the accesskey, overriding the automatic selection/generation from the UA. The `::accesskey`, if the parent allows it to generate, then wraps the child element.\r\n\r\nPossibly the \"default vs custom styling\" can be selected by the \"generate an ::accesskey\" property, rather than by `appearance` on the ::accesskey itself; an `auto` value applies UA styling and makes it ignore any author-level CSS, while `custom` applies no UA styling (or some mandated-by-spec styling, so it's predictable) and lets author-level CSS style it. This is equivalent functionality, just a question of what is more natural or easier for implementations.\r\n\r\nWhether or not it can select down into descendant text can go either way; I don't have a strong opinion on this, but lean toward \"let descendants provide the accesskey\".",
