    "updated_at": "2019-10-29T12:08:23Z",
    "body": "Let me make the possible scenarios XUL reportedly (I'll verify) supports a bit more concrete:\r\n\r\nMarkup | Expected rendering (changes in bold and italic)\r\n------------ | -------------\r\n`<el accesskey=a>Amazing</el>` | <b><i>A</i></b>mazing\r\n`<el accesskey=z>Amazing</el>` | Ama<b><i>z</i></b>ing\r\n`<el accesskey=1>Amazing</el>` | Amazing<b><i> (1)</i></b>\r\n`<el accesskey=1>Amazing:</el>` | Amazing<b><i> (1):</i></b>\r\n`<el accesskey=1>Amazing;</el>` | Amazing;<b><i> (1)</i></b>\r\n\r\nEffectively, there's two primitives:\r\n\r\n1. Find X in the string and if it's not there, append it in some manner\r\n2. Highlight X in the string\r\n\r\nLow-level support for this is rather tricky though and getting all the edge cases and bidi right might not be worth it.",
